1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,634
June thirtieth, nineteen oh eight, seven in the morning.

2
00:00:03,634 --> 00:00:09,502
A trader named Semenov sits on the porch of a remote outpost called Vanavara, facing north.

3
00:00:10,633 --> 00:00:13,768
Then, he says, the sky splits in two.

4
00:00:13,768 --> 00:00:20,037
Fire appears, high and wide, over the forest, in the direction he calls Onkoul's Tunguska Road.

5
00:00:21,166 --> 00:00:26,811
Nobody filmed what happened at Tunguska. Nothing like it had ever been captured on camera.

6
00:00:26,811 --> 00:00:33,208
But in twenty thirteen, a similar fireball exploded over Russia again, and this time, cameras were rolling.

7
00:00:34,333 --> 00:00:38,777
Back at Vanavara, the blast did something just as violent. Windows shattered.

8
00:00:38,777 --> 00:00:42,110
An iron lock on a barn snapped clean off.

9
00:00:42,110 --> 00:00:46,924
Semenov himself was thrown several meters off his own porch, and knocked unconscious.

10
00:00:48,066 --> 00:00:52,298
His account was recorded twenty two years later, by a Soviet expedition.

11
00:00:52,298 --> 00:00:57,940
Historians treat the shape of it as reliable. But it is a memory, not a photograph.

12
00:00:59,066 --> 00:01:02,249
No crater was ever found. No meteorite was ever recovered.

13
00:01:02,249 --> 00:01:07,660
And more than a century later, nobody has fully explained what tore through the sky that morning.

14
00:01:08,799 --> 00:01:10,445
You're watching mindwired.

15
00:01:11,566 --> 00:01:20,395
This is the story of the Tunguska event, the biggest explosion in recorded history, and the mystery that still has no crater, and no meteorite.

16
00:01:21,533 --> 00:01:24,459
Chapter one. The world before Tunguska.

17
00:01:25,599 --> 00:01:32,913
The Podkamennaya Tunguska River is a remote tributary of the Yenisei, cutting through more than a thousand miles of Siberian taiga.

18
00:01:34,033 --> 00:01:43,019
The nearest settlement was Vanavara, not a town, just a small trading post for Evenki hunters, about sixty five kilometers from what would become ground zero.

19
00:01:44,166 --> 00:01:45,699
This was permafrost country.

20
00:01:45,699 --> 00:01:50,680
Boggy and mosquito-ridden in its short summer, snow-covered the rest of the year.

21
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:52,212
No roads. No railways.

22
00:01:53,333 --> 00:02:00,741
The people who lived here were the Evenki, nomadic reindeer herders, organized by clan, with an animist, shamanic religion.

23
00:02:00,741 --> 00:02:03,860
The word 'shaman' itself comes from their language.

24
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,348
In nineteen oh eight, the idea that rocks could fall from outer space at all was itself barely a century old.

25
00:02:12,466 --> 00:02:20,668
In seventeen ninety four, the physicist Ernst Chladni first proposed it, against fierce resistance from scientists steeped in Newton and Aristotle.

26
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:34,234
It took a shower of thousands of stones over L'Aigle, France, in eighteen oh three, investigated for the French Academy by Jean-Baptiste Biot, to finally force the scientific establishment to accept it.

27
00:02:35,366 --> 00:02:38,319
By then, astronomers were also finding asteroids.

28
00:02:38,319 --> 00:02:42,537
Giuseppe Piazzi discovered the first, Ceres, in eighteen oh one.

29
00:02:42,537 --> 00:02:46,755
Photographic surveys from the eighteen nineties onward found hundreds more.

30
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:57,461
What did not exist yet, even in nineteen oh eight, was any concept of an object exploding in the air, leaving no crater, and no fragments, at all.

31
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:01,249
Even ordinary meteorite craters were still disputed.

32
00:03:01,249 --> 00:03:10,333
When a mining engineer named Daniel Barringer argued, in nineteen oh five, that Arizona's Meteor Crater was formed by a space impact, the U.S.

33
00:03:10,333 --> 00:03:16,389
Geological Survey's chief geologist rejected him for years, insisting it was a volcanic steam explosion instead.

34
00:03:17,533 --> 00:03:23,411
The man who would eventually chase this new kind of mystery down was a Russian mineralogist named Leonid Kulik.

35
00:03:24,533 --> 00:03:32,735
He fought in the First World War, was briefly imprisoned for revolutionary activity, and only afterward returned to science, in Tomsk and then Leningrad.

36
00:03:33,866 --> 00:03:36,635
Chapter two. The morning of June thirtieth.

37
00:03:37,766 --> 00:03:44,960
At approximately seven fifteen in the morning, local time, a massive explosion tore through the sky above the Tunguska basin.

38
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:48,917
Later back-calculations put it somewhere between seven fourteen and seven seventeen.

39
00:03:48,917 --> 00:03:51,794
There was no synchronized clock at the scene.

40
00:03:52,933 --> 00:04:04,537
Thirty kilometers from the blast, a young Evenki man named Chuchan, of the Shanyagir clan, described a light 'like a second sun,' and being knocked down again and again by the blast waves.

41
00:04:04,537 --> 00:04:09,812
His account was recorded eighteen years later, in nineteen twenty six, by a Soviet ethnographer.

42
00:04:09,812 --> 00:04:11,219
Memory, not a photograph.

43
00:04:12,366 --> 00:04:16,338
Across dozens of separate accounts, the same sequence repeats.

44
00:04:16,338 --> 00:04:18,544
Blinding heat first, almost instantly.

45
00:04:18,544 --> 00:04:24,722
Then, seconds to nearly a minute later, a shockwave that physically knocked people down.

46
00:04:25,866 --> 00:04:31,013
Seismographs in Irkutsk, Tashkent, Tbilisi, and Jena all registered the blast within hours.

47
00:04:31,013 --> 00:04:38,535
So did barographs as far away as Ditcham Park House, in England, over fifty six hundred kilometers from Siberia.

48
00:04:39,666 --> 00:04:44,233
For days afterward, the night sky stayed strangely bright across Europe and Asia.

49
00:04:44,233 --> 00:04:49,854
Bright enough, in Scotland, to read a newspaper, or play golf, at two in the morning.

50
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:53,256
Evenki herders reported losing everything.

51
00:04:53,256 --> 00:04:59,123
One man, Andrey Onkoul, reportedly lost his entire reindeer herd, without a trace.

52
00:05:00,266 --> 00:05:04,882
There are unconfirmed reports that as many as three Evenki herders may have died.

53
00:05:04,882 --> 00:05:12,465
But that traces only to oral testimony gathered in nineteen twenty six, eighteen years after the event, with no forensic evidence at all.

54
00:05:13,599 --> 00:05:16,503
The Evenki who survived had their own explanation.

55
00:05:16,503 --> 00:05:24,126
Some believed a shaman named Magankan had summoned the thunder-spirit Agdy, to destroy a rival clan camped in the blast's path.

56
00:05:25,266 --> 00:05:28,053
This was the world just after the blast.

57
00:05:28,053 --> 00:05:34,670
Testimony, awe, grief, and a story passed down for years before anyone from the outside world arrived to look.

58
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:38,386
Chapter three. The scale of the blast.

59
00:05:39,533 --> 00:05:47,102
When investigators finally reached the site, they found forest flattened across roughly two thousand one hundred fifty square kilometers.

60
00:05:47,102 --> 00:05:49,094
Eight hundred thirty square miles.

61
00:05:50,233 --> 00:06:00,029
The destruction radiated outward from a single point, in a butterfly-shaped pattern, up to thirty five kilometers from the center, every fallen tree pointing away from the blast.

62
00:06:01,166 --> 00:06:05,074
The popular figure is eighty million trees, flattened in an instant.

63
00:06:05,074 --> 00:06:07,561
But that number comes from early estimates.

64
00:06:07,561 --> 00:06:13,600
Newer tree-ring analysis suggests the real toll may be considerably lower, perhaps by a factor of four.

65
00:06:14,733 --> 00:06:24,607
The blast released an estimated ten to fifteen megatons of energy, roughly a thousand times the Hiroshima bomb, and on the scale of the nineteen eighty Mount Saint Helens eruption.

66
00:06:24,607 --> 00:06:27,899
Some models push that range as high as fifty megatons.

67
00:06:29,033 --> 00:06:34,935
Some seismic stations recorded ground tremors that later analysis compared to a magnitude five earthquake.

68
00:06:34,935 --> 00:06:36,509
That comparison is retrospective.

69
00:06:36,509 --> 00:06:41,624
The Richter scale itself would not be invented for another twenty seven years.

70
00:06:42,766 --> 00:06:46,037
Scattered among the flattened trees were small, bowl-shaped bogs.

71
00:06:46,037 --> 00:06:51,125
For a while, investigators wondered if one of these might be the missing crater.

72
00:06:52,266 --> 00:06:58,731
This remains, and it remained then, the largest cosmic impact ever witnessed by human beings.

73
00:06:58,731 --> 00:07:01,748
An entire forest, wiped flat, in seconds.

74
00:07:02,866 --> 00:07:05,635
Chapter four. Nineteen years of silence.

75
00:07:06,766 --> 00:07:11,596
No scientist reached the site for nineteen years. Partly, this was simple geography.

76
00:07:11,596 --> 00:07:16,797
No roads, no railways, permafrost bog, and only a narrow travel window each summer.

77
00:07:17,933 --> 00:07:19,311
Partly, it was history.

78
00:07:19,311 --> 00:07:26,546
The First World War, the Russian Revolution, and the Civil War that followed devoured Russian science for most of that decade.

79
00:07:26,546 --> 00:07:30,681
The same years that should have been Leonid Kulik's prime working career.

80
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:40,528
In nineteen twenty six, a Soviet official named Innokenty Suslov traveled into the region, a full year before Kulik, to organize the first Soviet councils among the Evenki.

81
00:07:40,528 --> 00:07:45,828
It was Suslov who first brought word of the story's scale back to the Academy of Sciences.

82
00:07:46,966 --> 00:07:51,354
Kulik himself first tried to reach the site in nineteen twenty one, and failed.

83
00:07:51,354 --> 00:07:56,683
He didn't actually make it until nineteen twenty seven, guided the final stretch by local Evenki hunters.

84
00:07:57,833 --> 00:08:00,031
What he found was extraordinary.

85
00:08:00,031 --> 00:08:13,219
An inner zone of scorched, branch-stripped trees, still standing, like a forest of telephone poles, ringed by a much wider band of forest, flattened, radiating outward from one central point.

86
00:08:14,366 --> 00:08:17,651
And nothing else. No crater. No recovered meteorite fragments.

87
00:08:17,651 --> 00:08:23,857
Several bowl-shaped bogs he suspected at first turned out to be much older than nineteen oh eight.

88
00:08:23,857 --> 00:08:28,237
One even had tree stumps growing inside it, predating the blast entirely.

89
00:08:29,366 --> 00:08:38,612
Kulik returned in nineteen twenty eight, again in nineteen twenty nine and nineteen thirty, and once more in nineteen thirty eight, this time photographing the fell-zone from the air.

90
00:08:38,612 --> 00:08:41,800
He never found a crater. He never found a meteorite.

91
00:08:42,933 --> 00:08:48,175
Soviet expeditions in the nineteen fifties and sixties, decades later, recovered something else.

92
00:08:48,175 --> 00:08:53,417
Microscopic silicate spheres, in the soil and tree resin, with unusual nickel content.

93
00:08:53,417 --> 00:08:58,659
Scientists call this evidence 'consistent with' a cosmic origin. Not proof of one.

94
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:03,305
By the time all this was done, one fact was undeniable.

95
00:09:03,305 --> 00:09:09,361
Whatever tore through the sky that morning had left behind everything, except the one thing anyone expected to find.

96
00:09:10,500 --> 00:09:13,739
Chapter five. The theories that didn't survive.

97
00:09:14,866 --> 00:09:19,187
With no crater and no meteorite, the case stayed open for decades.

98
00:09:19,187 --> 00:09:23,147
And into that vacuum came theories far stranger than an asteroid.

99
00:09:24,266 --> 00:09:30,759
In nineteen seventy three, physicists Jackson and Ryan proposed something startling, in the journal Nature.

100
00:09:30,759 --> 00:09:39,417
A tiny primordial black hole, weighing about a hundred sextillion kilograms, had passed straight through the Earth, entering over Siberia.

101
00:09:40,566 --> 00:09:42,236
It was refuted almost immediately.

102
00:09:42,236 --> 00:09:47,244
The hypothesis itself predicted a matching exit shockwave on the opposite side of the planet.

103
00:09:47,244 --> 00:09:48,580
Nobody ever found one.

104
00:09:48,580 --> 00:09:58,930
A nineteen seventy four rebuttal in Nature, and Stephen Hawking's own later work on black hole evaporation, argued an object that small couldn't have survived to reach Earth intact at all.

105
00:10:00,066 --> 00:10:08,356
In nineteen sixty five, physicists Cowan, Atluri, and Libby proposed matter and antimatter had collided and annihilated each other in the sky.

106
00:10:08,356 --> 00:10:13,632
It doesn't hold up. An annihilation like that would leave almost no debris behind.

107
00:10:13,632 --> 00:10:17,777
But real mineral and dust debris was recovered at the site.

108
00:10:18,900 --> 00:10:22,431
The alien-spacecraft idea traces to something even less scientific.

109
00:10:22,431 --> 00:10:31,456
A nineteen forty six Soviet science-fiction story, written by an engineer named Alexander Kazantsev, popularized decades later in a nineteen seventy six book.

110
00:10:31,456 --> 00:10:37,342
No physical evidence has ever supported it. It endures as pop culture. Not as science.

111
00:10:38,466 --> 00:10:39,471
Three famous theories.

112
00:10:39,471 --> 00:10:46,172
Three dead ends, each one clearly attributed, and each one clearly ruled out by the physical evidence on the ground.

113
00:10:47,300 --> 00:10:49,651
Chapter six. The real suspect.

114
00:10:50,800 --> 00:10:57,419
The mainstream scientific consensus, built up over decades of NASA and peer-reviewed modeling, is far less exotic.

115
00:10:57,419 --> 00:11:07,153
A small stony asteroid, somewhere between fifty and eighty meters across, entered the atmosphere at a shallow angle, traveling around twenty seven kilometers a second.

116
00:11:08,300 --> 00:11:13,279
Around five to ten kilometers above the ground, it detonated in mid-air.

117
00:11:13,279 --> 00:11:19,919
Because it never reached the surface intact, it left behind no crater, and no large fragments.

118
00:11:19,919 --> 00:11:22,824
Exactly what investigators found, ninety years later.

119
00:11:23,966 --> 00:11:30,245
Whether it was a stony asteroid or an icy comet fragment is still genuinely disputed among specialists.

120
00:11:30,245 --> 00:11:37,263
A widely-cited two thousand one statistical study put the odds at roughly eighty three percent asteroid, seventeen percent comet.

121
00:11:37,263 --> 00:11:39,848
A real estimate. Not a settled answer.

122
00:11:40,966 --> 00:11:46,371
The one thing almost everyone agrees on: whatever it was, it never reached the ground.

123
00:11:46,371 --> 00:11:51,415
Which makes one small lake, eight kilometers from the epicenter, a genuinely open question.

124
00:11:52,533 --> 00:12:04,654
In papers published between two thousand seven and twenty twelve, University of Bologna geologist Luca Gasperini proposed that Lake Cheko could be an impact crater, carved out by a surviving fragment of the object.

125
00:12:05,800 --> 00:12:17,874
Russian researchers coring the lakebed in twenty seventeen found sediment only around two hundred eighty years old at the deepest layer they sampled, which, if representative, would mean the lake predates the blast entirely.

126
00:12:17,874 --> 00:12:20,716
The dispute has never been resolved either way.

127
00:12:21,833 --> 00:12:25,656
So the honest answer, in the end, is a genuine one.

128
00:12:25,656 --> 00:12:30,522
Almost certainly an asteroid, quite possibly a comet, detonating in the air above Siberia.

129
00:12:30,522 --> 00:12:34,346
And one open argument about a lake that nobody has settled.

130
00:12:35,466 --> 00:12:38,392
Chapter seven. Chelyabinsk: the proof.

131
00:12:39,533 --> 00:12:47,411
On February fifteenth, twenty thirteen, the largest airburst since Tunguska happened again, this time over Chelyabinsk, Russia.

132
00:12:47,411 --> 00:12:52,046
This time, dashcams and security cameras caught the whole thing.

133
00:12:53,166 --> 00:12:59,943
Commonly cited around four hundred to five hundred kilotons of energy, though estimates vary meaningfully across different papers.

134
00:12:59,943 --> 00:13:04,085
Still only a fraction of Tunguska's estimated ten to fifty megatons.

135
00:13:05,233 --> 00:13:09,103
For the first time, scientists had real measured data.

136
00:13:09,103 --> 00:13:15,123
Shockwave speed, injury patterns, damage radius, to test decades of Tunguska-based airburst models against.

137
00:13:15,123 --> 00:13:22,004
The definitive analysis, led by Olga Popova, was published in the journal Science in twenty thirteen.

138
00:13:23,133 --> 00:13:33,416
Planetary scientist Natalia Artemieva, who co-authored the standard Tunguska-Chelyabinsk comparison paper, has pointed to one earlier bridge that made this kind of modeling possible at all.

139
00:13:33,416 --> 00:13:40,139
The comet Shoemaker-Levy Nine slamming into Jupiter, in nineteen ninety four, watched by telescopes around the world.

140
00:13:41,266 --> 00:13:47,155
Physicist Mark Boslough, of Sandia National Laboratories, personally visited the Tunguska site in two thousand eight.

141
00:13:47,155 --> 00:13:57,461
He has continued using the physics of this blast for modern research, including a twenty twenty five paper correcting an unrelated, since-retracted claim about a site in Jordan.

142
00:13:57,461 --> 00:14:01,877
That's Boslough's position specifically, not a settled consensus about that other site.

143
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,586
Chapter eight. Why it still matters.

144
00:14:06,733 --> 00:14:13,156
NASA's own David Morrison, a pioneer of near-Earth-object risk assessment who has studied this for decades, put it simply.

145
00:14:13,156 --> 00:14:17,213
Tunguska, he says, is the largest cosmic impact witnessed by modern humans.

146
00:14:17,213 --> 00:14:23,974
It also is characteristic of the sort of impact we are likely to have to protect against in the future.

147
00:14:25,100 --> 00:14:34,758
On January seventh, twenty sixteen, NASA formally established the Planetary Defense Coordination Office, created specifically to find and track hazardous near-Earth objects.

148
00:14:34,758 --> 00:14:40,904
Its entire mission traces back to airbursts on Tunguska's scale, as the reference threat.

149
00:14:42,033 --> 00:14:52,742
In January twenty eighteen, NASA's Planetary Defense office sponsored a fifty-person workshop at Ames Research Center, applying the same post-Chelyabinsk modeling tools to the nineteen oh eight event.

150
00:14:52,742 --> 00:14:56,949
It produced eight peer-reviewed papers, published together in July twenty nineteen.

151
00:14:58,066 --> 00:15:08,175
That same office later sponsored DART, the Double Asteroid Redirection Test, the first time humanity deliberately altered an asteroid's orbit, by slamming a spacecraft straight into it.

152
00:15:09,300 --> 00:15:11,308
Looking forward, the Vera C.

153
00:15:11,308 --> 00:15:20,951
Rubin Observatory's ongoing sky survey is expected to sharply increase how many Tunguska and Chelyabinsk-sized objects we can spot, before they arrive, not after.

154
00:15:22,100 --> 00:15:28,707
For over a century, Tunguska has stood as planetary science's working definition of the worst realistic case.

155
00:15:28,707 --> 00:15:34,926
A city-flattening blast, that under current detection rates, would arrive with effectively no warning at all.

156
00:15:36,066 --> 00:15:39,888
What stays with me about Tunguska isn't the size of the blast.

157
00:15:39,888 --> 00:15:41,799
It's the shape of the mystery.

158
00:15:41,799 --> 00:15:45,940
How something this large could happen, and still leave so little evidence behind.

159
00:15:47,066 --> 00:15:54,826
Every theory that tried to explain it with something exotic, a black hole, antimatter, a spacecraft, fell apart against ordinary evidence.

160
00:15:54,826 --> 00:16:02,217
And the real answer, an asteroid or a comet detonating in the air, turned out unsettling enough on its own.

161
00:16:03,366 --> 00:16:09,485
We now know this kind of blast can happen again, anywhere, with almost no warning.

162
00:16:09,485 --> 00:16:13,972
Chelyabinsk proved that, in real time, on camera, in twenty thirteen.

163
00:16:15,100 --> 00:16:18,809
More than a century later, the meteorite has still never been found.

164
00:16:19,933 --> 00:16:31,166
If a nineteen forty six science-fiction story convinced people aliens were behind Tunguska, wait until you hear the pilot who swore, on the record, that what he saw was not from this world.

165
00:16:32,300 --> 00:16:34,102
Subscribe to mindwired.

